- quickjs-unary-not-overload-abort: applying unary `~` (bitwise NOT) to *any*
  object whose [Symbol.operatorSet] resolves to a non-primitive operator set
  crashes the whole process with abort() - not a catchable JS exception -
  regardless of whether that operator set defines a `~` handler at all. Root
  cause is in the vendored ../quickjs/quickjs.c (a separate project/repo this
  one builds against, not qjs-modules' own code): js_not_slow()
  (quickjs.c:12730) calls js_call_unary_op_fallback() (quickjs.c:12421) for
  every OP_not, which unconditionally calls get_ovop_from_opcode(OP_not)
  (quickjs.c:12189-12214) before ever checking whether the operator set has
  a `~` entry - and that function's switch has no `case OP_not:` at all, so
  it falls through to `default: abort();`. doc/operator-overloading.md's own
  operator table lists `~` / `JS_OVOP_NOT` as a supported overloadable
  unary operator ("bitwise NOT") - that entry is simply wrong for this
  vendored quickjs.c, at least as currently built.
  Found while trying to wire lib/parser.js's unary `~` up to a Kleene-star
  combinator (ZeroOrMore) - confirmed not specific to Rule/lib/parser.js by
  reproducing the same crash against an unrelated Predicate instance (from
  quickjs-predicate.c, already shipped) with no `~` handler defined for it
  either. This means *any* class in this codebase that installs a
  Symbol.operatorSet (Predicate, and now Rule/Terminal/OneOrMore/Optional/
  Sequence/ZeroOrMore in lib/parser.js) is already exposed to this crash the
  moment user code applies unary `~` to an instance of it - not limited to
  code that deliberately tries to overload `~` itself. Left unfixed: the fix
  belongs in ../quickjs/quickjs.c (add a `case OP_not: return JS_OVOP_NOT;`
  to get_ovop_from_opcode(), matching the JS_OVOP_NOT enum value that
  already exists at quickjs.c:708 and is already listed in
  js_overloadable_operator_names - the pieces are all there except this one
  switch case), which is out of scope for qjs-modules itself.

    import { Predicate } from 'predicate';
    const p = Predicate.type(0);
    ~p;
    // expected: TypeError (no overloaded operator ~) or 0/-1 (no-op numeric
    // bitwise-not fallback), since Predicate's operator set has no '~' entry
    // actual: process aborts (SIGABRT), no JS-catchable exception at all

- sockets-default-export-undeclared: js_sockets_init (quickjs-
  sockets.c:3107-3111) unconditionally runs js_setmoduleexport(ctx, m,
  "default", socket_ctor) when the module is loaded as "socket", but the
  matching js_addmoduleexport(ctx, m, "default") is commented out (quickjs-
  sockets.c:3145). pre-existing (predates the 2026-07-20 dbuf_claim commit).

    import Socket from 'socket';
    console.log(Socket);
    // likely undefined or ub -- export slot was never declared

- test-path-stream-preexisting-failures: tests/test_path.js's `skipSeparator()`,
  `isSeparator()`, `isin()`, `equal()`, `toArray()` tests fail, and
  `equal()`/`toArray()` additionally throw "not a function" (suggesting a
  path.js API those tests expect no longer exists under that name);
  tests/test_stream.js's `tee(): both branches independently observe every
  chunk` test also throws "not a function". These assertion failures were
  always there, just never surfaced via exit code until tinytest.js's run()
  (exported as `tests`) was fixed to call std.exit(1) on any test failure
  (tests/tinytest.js:68-75) - before that, every consumer called
  `tests({...})` fire-and-forget at module scope without awaiting the
  returned promise, and run() itself never propagated failure past its own
  console output, so the qjsm process always exited 0 regardless of how many
  assertions failed, silently passing `ctest`. Left unfixed: out of scope
  (neither path.js/stream.js nor their tests have been touched to investigate
  the "not a function" root cause).

    ctest --test-dir build/<arch> -R 'test_path|test_stream'
    # expected: both FAIL, matching the failures their own console output
    # already reports
    # actual: both FAIL (ctest correctly surfaces this now - previously both
    # reported PASSED despite printing "N out of 41 tests failed" to stdout)

- quickjs-stack-size-flag-ineffective: qjsm's `--stack-size n` CLI option (and
  the underlying JS_SetMaxStackSize(), quickjs.c:2408-2412) has no observable
  effect on how deep JS recursion can actually go before "stack overflow" is
  thrown - the effective limit is a constant ~164 nested calls of a trivial
  `function f(n){ return n<=0 ? 0 : 1+f(n-1); }`, identical whether
  `--stack-size` is omitted, set to 10000, or set to 500000000. Binary-searched
  the exact threshold at several flag values (10000, 20000, 50000, 256000,
  5000000, 500000000, and unset) and got 164 every time - ruling out
  under/overflow of the strtod() parse or a too-small value by accident.
  update_stack_limit() (quickjs.c:2399-2406) recomputes rt->stack_limit from
  the *already-captured* rt->stack_top and the new rt->stack_size, which looks
  correct on paper, so either js_check_stack_overflow() (quickjs.c:1648-1664)
  isn't actually gating the interpreter's function-call path the way it
  appears to, or something else in this build (debug/sanitizer flags? a much
  larger per-JS_CallInternal-frame C stack footprint than usual?) is causing a
  real C-level stack exhaustion (caught via SIGSEGV/guard page and reported as
  a catchable "stack overflow", rather than js_check_stack_overflow's own
  proactive check firing first) well before rt->stack_limit would ever be
  reached - 8MB (`ulimit -s`) / 164 frames implies roughly 50KB of C stack per
  JS call frame, which is abnormally large for a single trivial recursive
  function call. Found while running lib/parser.js's Rule-based recursive-
  descent matching against a grammar compiled from tests/Shell-Grammar.y
  (tests/test_grammar.js) - a moderately nested grammar (if/then/else,
  ~15 chained non-terminals deep, tried 3x for condition/then-branch/else-
  branch) hits this same ~164-frame ceiling and throws "stack overflow" with
  no way to raise it via the documented `--stack-size` flag. Worked around in
  tests/test_grammar.js by using a shallower fantasy script (a pipeline and a
  `;`-separated command list, not an if/then/else) that stays within the
  ~164-frame budget; left unfixed here since the actual defect (if it's not
  simply an artifact of this build's compiler flags) is in the vendored
  ../quickjs/quickjs.c, out of scope for qjs-modules itself.

    function f(n) { return n <= 0 ? 0 : 1 + f(n - 1); }
    console.log(f(150));                 // 150, fine
    console.log(f(200));                 // expected: 200 (well under 256KB
                                          // default/any --stack-size budget)
                                          // actual: uncaught "stack overflow"
                                          // - and passing e.g.
                                          // `--stack-size 500000000` first
                                          // changes nothing

- wasm-node-glue-fetch-crash: qjsm.wasm's Emscripten-generated glue
  (build/wasm32-unknown-emscripten/qjsm.js, emitted by emsdk's bundled
  emscripten 2.0.10) picks the wasm-loading path in `instantiateAsync()`
  by checking only `typeof fetch === 'function'`, with no
  `ENVIRONMENT_IS_NODE` guard (qjsm.js:1534-1554). Node 18+ ships a global
  `fetch`, so under any modern Node this branch runs `fetch(wasmBinaryFile,
  ...)` with a plain filesystem path instead of the Node-native
  `readFileSync` path a few lines below, and `fetch()` throws immediately
  because the argument isn't a URL. This is a known compatibility gap
  between old Emscripten releases and Node's newer global fetch, fixed in
  more recent Emscripten by adding the environment check; upgrading emsdk
  (or patching the generated glue) would fix it properly. Found while
  smoke-testing qjsm.wasm after enabling QUICKJS_INTERNAL for the wasm
  build. Workaround below unblocks manual testing but isn't a real fix.

    node -e "delete globalThis.fetch;
             process.argv=[process.argv[0],'qjsm.js','-e','console.log(1)'];
             require('./qjsm.js')"
    // without the `delete globalThis.fetch`: TypeError: Failed to parse
    // URL from qjsm.wasm
    // with it: prints 1 as expected

- wasm-qjsm-no-real-file-imports: qjsm.wasm has no filesystem wired up to
  the host when run under Node (`-sNODERAWFS` was never set, see
  ../CMakeLists.txt:43-68, and no `--preload-file`/NODEFS mount is done
  either) -- only `MEMFS`, which starts empty. Every native/JS builtin
  module registered via BUILTINS_NATIVE/BUILTINS_COMPILED loads fine
  (`--list` shows all 27 native + 14 compiled-JS modules, and `-e` can
  import and use them), but `-m <realfile.js>` for any file that actually
  lives on disk fails because that file was never copied into MEMFS. This
  means qjsm.wasm today can only run inline `-e` scripts (or scripts naming
  a builtin module by name) under Node, not arbitrary local .js files --
  the primary way qjs-modules' own lib/*.js wrappers are normally loaded.
  Found in the same wasm smoke-test session as the entry above.

    // From build/wasm32-unknown-emscripten, with a file test.js sitting
    // right next to qjsm.js/qjsm.wasm:
    node -e "delete globalThis.fetch;
             process.argv=[process.argv[0],'qjsm.js','-m','test.js'];
             require('./qjsm.js')"
    // expected: test.js runs
    // actual: ReferenceError: could not load module 'test.js': ReferenceError:
    //         could not load module filename 'test.js'
